# Environment variables for the sc Heerenveen Fansite.
#
# Copy this file to .env in the repository root and fill in the values. Git ignores .env, but not this file, so never
# put a real value here.
#
# Every variable that the site or the importer reads is listed below, with a line on what it's for. When you add one,
# add it here too, and to the app's schema for parseEnv (from @fansite/shared/env), which checks them at startup.

# Which environment the site runs in: local, acceptance or production. Only production may appear in search engines; the
# others send a noindex header and a robots.txt that refuses everything. The site doesn't start without it.
SITE_ENV=local

# The MariaDB database, as mysql://user:password@host:port/database. The site reads the source register from it, runs the
# migrations on it when it starts, and doesn't start without it. With SITE_ENV=acceptance or production it also puts the
# real sources from the seed in, and on acceptance it takes the test sources out. The value below is for the database
# that `docker compose up -d --wait` starts on your own computer: it has no password and only listens on that computer.
# The tests make their own database on this server, so the user must be allowed to create databases.
DATABASE_URL=mysql://root@127.0.0.1:3306/fansite

# In a git worktree the scripts replace the database `fansite` with `fansite_<name of the folder>`, and give the folder
# its own ports, so sessions that work next to each other don't share them (see packages/db/README.md). Set a variable
# yourself, in the environment or here, and that value wins: DATABASE_URL with another database than `fansite`, or
# SITE_PORT, STORYBOOK_PORT, SMOKE_PORT, E2E_PORT, E2E_WITHOUT_DATABASE_PORT, FAKE_MAIL_PORT. WORKTREE_NAME chooses
# another name than the folder's, and an empty one switches this off. Leave them out normally.
# WORKTREE_NAME=

# Where `pnpm db:backup` puts its dumps of the database: an absolute path, outside the repository and outside public_html.
# The folder is made with rights for your own account only, and the command refuses a folder that others can read. On the
# host cron runs the same command, with the value in the file next to DATABASE_URL (see docs/back-ups.md). Nothing else
# reads it.
# BACKUP_FOLDER=/Users/you/fansite-backups
# How many days of dumps the command keeps (1 to 365), 14 if you leave it out. It removes the older ones of the same
# database after a dump that worked.
# BACKUP_KEEP_DAYS=14
# The programs the backup and restore commands start, if they aren't on the path: `mysqldump` and `mysql`, or the MariaDB
# ones, `mariadb-dump` and `mariadb`.
# MYSQLDUMP_COMMAND=mysqldump
# MYSQL_COMMAND=mysql

# The mail server that sendMail uses for the contact form and the login link, and the address that mail comes from. The
# first four are the settings of the mailbox on the host: its server, the port (465 starts with TLS, any other port, such
# as 587, must switch to it), the user name (the whole address) and the password. MAIL_FROM is the sender: an address, or a
# name followed by an address in < >. On acceptance and production all five are required, and the site doesn't start
# without them. Locally leave them empty: sendMail then writes the mail in the log of `pnpm dev` and sends nothing. Fill in
# all five or none. Never use the real password on your own computer.
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASSWORD=
MAIL_FROM=

# Where the importer mails when the same import fails three runs in a row (a feed, the matches or the squad of a season,
# or the nightly backup), at most once a day while it lasts, and once more when it works again. One address, such as
# you@example.org, or a name followed by an address in < >. It goes through the mailbox above, so it does nothing
# without it. Leave it empty and the importer writes a line in its log and carries on. Locally leave it empty.
ALERT_MAIL_TO=

# The branch that `pnpm review` shows in the tab of the site and in the sidebar of Storybook. That command sets it for the
# dev servers it starts; you never set it yourself. Only `next dev` and `storybook dev` show it, so acceptance, production
# and the Storybook on ds.heerenveen.org never do, whatever this holds.
# REVIEW_BRANCH=
